<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh of Ibelin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130–<a href="/years/1169">1169</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">House of Ibelin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh of Ibelin was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-of-ibelin">Barisan of Ibelin</a> and lord of Ibelin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. He was captured by Nur ad-Din's forces in 1157 and ransomed, and he married Agnes of Courtenay, former wife of King Amalric. He died on pilgrimage around 1169, and his brothers Baldwin and Balian inherited his lands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hugh was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-of-ibelin">Barisan of Ibelin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvis-of-ramla">Helvis of Ramla</a>. He was old enough to witness charters in 1148, as was his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-of-ibelin">Baldwin of Ibelin</a>, and since the male age of majority was fifteen this suggests that he was born in the early 1130s. The historian H.E. Mayer has suggested that a limited competence may have been accepted from the age of eight, but his examples come from the royal house, whose situation was somewhat different. Hugh was probably about ten years older than his youngest brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a>.</p><p>After Barisan died in 1150, Helvis married Manasses of Hierges, the constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and one of the strongest supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Melisende</a> in her power struggle against her son, King Baldwin III. When Baldwin won that struggle in 1152, Manasses was exiled, and Hugh inherited the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> from his mother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hugh took part in the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> in 1153. In 1157 he was captured in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a>, and he was probably released the next year. In 1159 he visited the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and met the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Comnenus</a>, who had come to assert his suzerainty over the principality. In 1163 he married Agnes of Courtenay, whom Amalric had been forced to divorce before becoming king that year. Some accounts hold that Agnes had already been betrothed or married to Hugh before 1157, and that she married Amalric only after Hugh was taken prisoner.</p><p>In 1167 Hugh joined Amalric's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was responsible for building a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. The crusaders allied with the sultan against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, the general of Nur ad-Din Zangi, who was also fighting for control of Egypt, and Hugh was sent with the sultan's son Kamil to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He was the first crusader to see the sultan's palace. At the siege of Bilbeis during the same campaign, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">Ibelin family</a> tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-milly">Philip of Milly</a> saved Hugh's life after he broke his leg and fell under his horse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hugh died around 1169 while on a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>. His marriage to Agnes of Courtenay had produced no children, and his lordships of Ibelin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> stayed in his family. The name was borne again in the next generations of the family: a later Hugh of Ibelin, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-the-old-lord-of-beirut">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</a>, was his grand-nephew.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hugh of Ibelin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
